{
  "slug": "all-o-one-data-structure",
  "title": "All O`one Data Structure",
  "topic": "data-structures",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "design / hash map plus doubly linked list of count buckets",
  "statement": "Simulate a counter store that keeps one count per integer key and can report a key holding the largest or the smallest count. Two parallel lists of the same length describe the run: ops and keys, where entry i is the operation performed at step i.\n\n1: increment the count of key keys[i], starting the key at count 1 when it is not tracked.\n2: decrement the count of key keys[i]. A key that is not tracked is left alone, and a key whose count would fall to 0 is dropped from the store.\n3: record the tracked key whose count is largest.\n4: record the tracked key whose count is smallest.\n\nkeys[i] is ignored for operations 3 and 4. Record -1 when the store is empty at the moment a query runs. When several keys share the extreme count, record the smallest of those key values, so every answer is unambiguous.\n\nReturn the recorded query answers in the order the queries appeared.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= ops.length <= 200` and `1 <= keys.length <= 200` and `1 <= ops[i] <= 4` and `1 <= keys[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "ops = [1], keys = [1]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "ops = [2, 4], keys = [50]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(ops: number[], keys: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "A map from key to count makes every update trivial, but a query that scans the whole map is the part that costs; find a way to stop scanning.",
    "Group the keys by count and link those groups in increasing count order; the two extremes are then the ends of one list.",
    "Since a count moves by one at a time, a key always steps into the neighbouring group, so nothing has to be searched, only linked and unlinked, and a group that loses its last key must be removed."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force keeps a plain map from key to count, which makes an update O(1) but forces every query to walk all n tracked keys to find the extreme count and break ties by the smallest key, so queries cost O(n) each. The optimal adds a doubly linked list of buckets sorted by increasing count: each bucket owns the set of keys sharing one count, and a map from key to its bucket. Updates move a key to the neighbouring bucket, because a count only changes by one, and the smallest and largest counts sit permanently at the head and the tail of the list.",
    "why_optimal": "With u updates and q queries the brute force pays O(u + q * n), and the n keys must all be visible to a query only because the extremes are stored nowhere in particular. Bucketing fixes that: the head and tail are the extremes by construction, so a query touches only the keys that share the extreme count, giving O(u + q * k) where k is that group's size. The update itself needs no scan at all, since the destination bucket is always the neighbour of the current one and can be reused when it already holds the right count. The residual k factor comes only from the tie-break rule, and it is bounded by the constraint that keys live between 1 and 100.",
    "pitfalls": "Leaving empty buckets in the list after a key moves out, so the head or the tail points at a bucket with no keys and queries return nothing. Creating a fresh bucket with the same count as the neighbouring one instead of reusing it, which splits a count group in two and breaks the invariant that the list is strictly increasing. Forgetting to delete the key from the key-to-bucket map when a decrement takes its count to zero, which leaves a phantom entry pointing at a detached bucket. Detaching a bucket before finishing with it, losing the next pointer needed for the move. Returning -1 for an empty store but a stale answer for a store that became empty only after the last decrement."
  },
